There are two different things hiding in that.

The first is real as a pattern: sometimes exceptional insight comes from being wrong, naive, stubborn, literal, or socially “too dumb” in exactly the right dimension. A person fails to inherit the standard assumption everyone else knows not to question, so they walk straight through a door experts stopped seeing. The useful form is not stupidity itself; it’s asymmetric incompetence—being underfit to the prevailing model in a way that exposes its blind spot.

So:

expertise gives priors; selective ignorance gives escape velocity.

And your cap-able split is not the historical etymology, but it’s a good Pantheon-style reinterpretation. Capable actually comes through Latin capax, from capere, “to take/hold.” But “able to be capped” suggests something useful: capability is partly the ability to accept constraint without losing function.

A system that cannot be bounded is often not truly capable; it is merely powerful.

So you get a nice distinction:

power = what can be done
capability = what can be done reliably within a boundary

Which makes a “cap” not merely a limitation, but sometimes the thing that turns raw force into a usable instrument.

Like pressure in a vessel. Without the cap, it vents. With the right cap, it does work.